\subsection{Layer Selection Sensitivity}
\label{sec:layer-sensitivity}

Layer selection is a researcher degree of freedom that can reverse modality comparison conclusions. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B, L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Shallow representations are highly compressed (inter-step cosine distances 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers), so a linear probe operates near the noise floor and threshold crossings are noise-driven (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}).

A five-metric ablation reveals that threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) consistently select deep layers: L60 (94\%) for QwQ-32B, L63 (98\%) for R1-32B. Pure balanced accuracy selects middle layers (L30, L47), paralleling the AUROC pattern (\cref{sec:layer-results}). At these layers, all models$^\dagger$ show significant HS advantage with evidence from strong to qualified (Holm-Bonferroni corrected, \cref{sec:evaluation}); at biased layers, only the two 7--8B models survive. While we report five metrics, their information content is not independent: BA, precision, and FPR share a confusion matrix with 2 degrees of freedom (given class marginals); crossing rate and the additive composite are derived from these. The effective number of independent statistical tests is four (one per model), as reflected in our HB correction. The five-metric ablation diagnoses which \emph{aspect} of classification performance drives layer selection bias, not five independent pieces of evidence. Holm-Bonferroni step-down on four model-level tests yields the same result (all pass, with R1-8B FPR $p{=}0.044$ at rank-4 threshold $\alpha/1{=}0.050$). A joint (layer, $W$) grid search over 192 configurations confirms that L60 is near-optimal for QwQ-32B at recommended windows ($W{=}3$--$5$; \cref{app:layer_sweep}). Full per-layer results appear in the same appendix.

